{
  "_doc": "DISPLAY FINISHES for every mixer screen (tracker §4.16 D2). A finish is the physical construction of a display — what emits the picture and what you look at it through — kept SEPARATE from what the picture shows (that is the scope's _Mode) and from what color the signal is (that is the WaveformPalette). A layout node says {\"finish\": \"glass.amber\", \"mode\": 0} and may override any single key on top; ScopeDisplay merges finish first, node second. Read by ScopeFinishes.cs. Add a finish here and every display can wear it — no shader edit, no layout surgery.",

  "_surfaceModes": "0 none · 1 glass · 2 plastic · 3 LED matrix · 4 LCD",

  "_reflections": "REFLECTIONS ARE NOT AUTHORED HERE. They are computed per-fragment from the three scene lamps (UiSceneDirector's rig) in CG/Core/UIDisplaySurface.cginc, exactly like every knob and button. surfaceMode alone decides what the cover is MADE of — glass is sharp and clear, plastic is broad and broken, an LED cover is semi-gloss, an LCD has a polariser sheen — and that is usually the only key a finish needs. The old sheenAmount/sheenAngle/sheenWidth/sheenPos and plasticAmount keys are removed: they painted a stationary highlight at a fixed angle, which is why these screens used to be the only things on the panel that ignored the lights.",

  "_reflectionKeys": "Optional per-finish MULTIPLIERS on the mode's natural material, all defaulting to 1 (= correct for that mode), 0 = off: surfaceReflect (overall strength) · surfaceGloss (highlight tightness) · surfaceRough (microfacet break-up) · surfaceFresnel (edge reflectivity) · surfaceCurveOptical (dome of the cover — this is the GLASS's shape, distinct from curveAmount which warps the PICTURE) · surfaceEnv (reflected-room brightness) · surfaceInnerShadow (bezel lip occlusion cast onto the face). Plus surfaceSignalMask 0..1 (default 0.6) — how hard a bright signal suppresses reflection over itself, so glare never buries the curve — and surfaceEnvColor, the colour of the room being reflected.",

